  {
    "topicTitle": "Application Resources",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-1",
        "title": "Argo CD Application Custom Resource Architecture",
        "content": "# Argo CD Application Custom Resource Architecture\n\n## Purpose and architecture\nThe Argo CD Application is a Kubernetes custom resource that describes the relationship between a source repository and a destination cluster. It identifies the repository URL, target revision, application path, destination server, namespace, and synchronization policy. The Application object is declarative configuration; it does not itself run containers or create workloads.\n\n## Important specification fields\nThe `spec.source` or `spec.sources` field identifies Git, Helm, or another supported configuration source. `spec.destination` selects the cluster and namespace. `spec.project` associates the application with an AppProject for authorization boundaries. `spec.syncPolicy` controls automated reconciliation, pruning, and self-healing behavior.\n\n## Implementation\nCreate an Application manifest with an explicit project, immutable or controlled Git revision, repository path, destination namespace, and carefully selected sync options. Keep production and non-production destinations separate. Store the manifest in a controlled repository so changes to application definitions are reviewed like application code.\n\n## Verification and failure analysis\nUse `kubectl get applications -n argocd` and `argocd app get <application>` to inspect the object and controller-reported status. A missing destination, invalid project, inaccessible repository, or incorrect source path can prevent reconciliation. Inspect the Application conditions and controller logs before changing cluster resources manually."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-2",
        "title": "Git Repository Sources and Revision Resolution",
        "content": "# Git Repository Sources and Revision Resolution\n\n## Repository source model\nAn Argo CD application source connects a desired-state repository to a rendering process. The repository URL identifies the remote source, `targetRevision` selects a branch, tag, or commit, and `path` identifies the directory containing Kubernetes manifests or a configuration tool definition. These values determine exactly which desired resources Argo CD calculates.\n\n## Revision selection\nBranches are convenient for continuous delivery but move as commits are added. Tags provide release-oriented references, while commit SHAs provide precise reproducibility. A production deployment that follows a moving branch can change after a new commit without an operator editing the Application object.\n\n## Implementation and security\nRegister private repositories with an appropriate credential method and least-privilege access. Avoid embedding passwords or tokens in manifests. Separate application source repositories from platform configuration where ownership and access requirements differ. Validate repository connectivity and ensure the selected revision exists.\n\n## Troubleshooting\nA failed comparison may report repository authentication errors, an unknown revision, an unavailable remote, or a missing path. Check repository registration, credentials, DNS, network egress, and the exact revision. Use `argocd app get <application>` and repository-related controller logs to identify whether failure occurs during fetch, checkout, or manifest generation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-3",
        "title": "Helm Chart Sources and Values Rendering",
        "content": "# Helm Chart Sources and Values Rendering\n\n## Chart source architecture\nArgo CD can render Helm charts from a Git repository or a Helm chart repository. The chart source identifies the chart location and version, while values files and inline parameters customize the rendered Kubernetes resources. Helm rendering produces manifests for Argo CD to compare with the live cluster; Argo CD normally owns synchronization rather than relying on a continuously running Helm release process.\n\n## Values precedence\nValues may come from chart defaults, one or more values files, inline values, and explicit parameters. The effective result depends on the configured source and precedence rules. Environment-specific values should be separated so development, staging, and production differences remain reviewable.\n\n## Practical implementation\nPin chart versions for controlled releases. Keep shared defaults in the chart and environment overrides in separate files. Validate the rendered output with Helm tooling before promoting a chart change. Avoid placing credentials directly in values files stored in an unrestricted repository.\n\n## Verification and failure analysis\nInspect generated manifests when a chart fails to render. Common causes include a missing chart version, incorrect values-file path, invalid YAML, unsupported chart schema, or a value with the wrong type. Compare rendered output between revisions and verify that a chart upgrade does not unintentionally replace persistent storage, Services, or security settings."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-4",
        "title": "Kustomize Bases, Overlays, and Environment Patches",
        "content": "# Kustomize Bases, Overlays, and Environment Patches\n\n## Configuration composition\nKustomize builds Kubernetes manifests from a base and one or more overlays. A base contains reusable resources, while an overlay applies environment-specific patches, labels, namespace settings, image versions, or additional resources. Argo CD can point an Application source at a Kustomize directory and render the resulting configuration.\n\n## Environment separation\nA development overlay may use lower replica counts and test endpoints. A production overlay may define multiple replicas, stricter resource requests, production ingress, and stronger security settings. Shared configuration should remain in the base; differences should be explicit in overlays rather than hidden in manual cluster edits.\n\n## Implementation workflow\nKeep each overlay under a clear directory such as `overlays/dev`, `overlays/stage`, and `overlays/prod`. Use `kustomization.yaml` to reference resources and patches. Test each overlay with `kubectl kustomize` or `kustomize build` before allowing Argo CD to synchronize it.\n\n## Troubleshooting\nA build failure can result from an invalid resource reference, patch target mismatch, deprecated field, duplicate resource identity, or incorrect relative path. Inspect the rendered output for unexpected namespaces, image tags, or resource names. If an overlay unexpectedly changes many resources, compare its output with the base and review each patch independently."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-5",
        "title": "Destination Cluster Registration and Namespace Boundaries",
        "content": "# Destination Cluster Registration and Namespace Boundaries\n\n## Destination model\nAn Argo CD Application destination identifies where desired resources should be applied. It normally specifies a cluster API server and a namespace. The cluster must be registered with Argo CD, and the controller must have valid network access and Kubernetes permissions to perform the requested operations.\n\n## Cluster registration\nCluster credentials and connection information allow Argo CD to communicate with a target Kubernetes API. Registration should use a dedicated identity with only the permissions required for the applications managed by that Argo CD installation. Production clusters should not receive broad administrative permissions merely to simplify deployment.\n\n## Namespace boundaries\nThe destination namespace provides an important workload boundary, but it is not a complete security boundary by itself. Kubernetes RBAC, network policies, quotas, admission controls, and AppProject restrictions must also be considered. Namespace creation behavior should be deliberate and controlled through the Application configuration.\n\n## Operational verification\nCheck the registered cluster with `argocd cluster list`, inspect its connection status, and verify the destination server and namespace in the Application specification. Connection failures may arise from expired credentials, API endpoint changes, firewall rules, DNS, or certificate trust problems. Permission failures require reviewing the target cluster role bindings and service identity."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-6",
        "title": "AppProject Policy and Application Isolation",
        "content": "# AppProject Policy and Application Isolation\n\n## Why AppProjects matter\nAn Argo CD AppProject groups applications under a policy boundary. It can restrict which source repositories applications may use, which destination clusters and namespaces they may target, and which Kubernetes resource kinds they may create. The default project is convenient for initial evaluation but is often too permissive for a multi-team production platform.\n\n## Policy design\nCreate separate projects for teams, environments, or trust boundaries. Allow only approved repository URLs and destinations. Restrict cluster-scoped resources when application teams should manage only namespace-scoped workloads. Review project roles and their permissions as carefully as Kubernetes RBAC.\n\n## Implementation\nDefine AppProject resources declaratively and manage them through a controlled platform repository. Ensure each Application references the intended project. Test allowed and denied source, destination, and resource combinations before onboarding application teams.\n\n## Troubleshooting and governance\nAn Application may become invalid when its repository or destination is not permitted by its project. Inspect the AppProject specification and Application conditions rather than bypassing the restriction. A project policy change can affect multiple applications, so review its impact and use a staged rollout with an explicit rollback plan."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-7",
        "title": "Automated Synchronization and Reconciliation Policy",
        "content": "# Automated Synchronization and Reconciliation Policy\n\n## Reconciliation behavior\nArgo CD continuously compares the desired manifests rendered from the configured source with live Kubernetes objects. Manual synchronization requires an operator or automation to approve an application of the desired state. Automated synchronization allows Argo CD to apply eligible changes when the comparison detects an out-of-sync state.\n\n## Policy controls\nAutomated sync can be combined with pruning and self-healing, but these controls have different consequences. Pruning removes resources that are no longer declared. Self-healing attempts to correct live changes that differ from Git. Configure these options according to application risk, ownership, and recovery requirements.\n\n## Production rollout\nStart with manual synchronization for unfamiliar workloads. Introduce automated synchronization after manifest validation, health checks, and rollback procedures are established. Use protected branches, pull-request review, and promotion controls so a bad commit cannot silently become a production deployment.\n\n## Verification\nInspect sync status, operation history, and Application conditions. Confirm that the intended revision was applied and that health checks completed. If automated sync does not run, examine sync windows, project restrictions, failed previous operations, and Application policy. Do not assume that an OutOfSync status always means automatic deployment is permitted."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-8",
        "title": "Pruning, Resource Deletion, and Orphan Protection",
        "content": "# Pruning, Resource Deletion, and Orphan Protection\n\n## Resource lifecycle\nPruning removes managed Kubernetes resources when they are no longer present in the desired manifests. This makes Git-based deletion meaningful, but it also creates risk: removing a manifest can remove a live Service, Deployment, or persistent workload. Pruning behavior must be understood before enabling it in a production application.\n\n## Safe deletion controls\nReview deletion-sensitive resources separately from routine application updates. PersistentVolumeClaims, databases, and shared infrastructure may require retention policies or independent ownership. Argo CD resource tracking and deletion propagation influence how managed objects are identified and removed. Finalizers can delay deletion while cleanup completes.\n\n## Orphan detection\nOrphaned resources are objects in an application namespace that are not tracked by the Application. They may be leftovers from an old release, manually created resources, or objects intentionally managed by another controller. Investigate ownership before deleting them.\n\n## Operational procedure\nReview the diff and sync plan before enabling pruning. Use explicit resource exclusions or preservation controls where justified, and test deletion behavior in a non-production environment. During an incident, identify the responsible Application and resource tracking metadata before taking action. Never delete an unexplained orphan merely to make the dashboard appear clean."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-9",
        "title": "Self-Healing and Live-State Drift Management",
        "content": "# Self-Healing and Live-State Drift Management\n\n## Desired state versus live state\nConfiguration drift occurs when a live Kubernetes resource differs from the version rendered from Git. Drift can be caused by manual edits, another automation system, admission controllers, or a controller that changes fields after creation. Argo CD reports differences so operators can understand whether the cluster still matches the intended configuration.\n\n## Self-healing behavior\nWhen enabled with automated synchronization, self-healing allows Argo CD to correct eligible live changes by applying the desired configuration. This is useful for preventing unauthorized or accidental manual changes, but it can also overwrite emergency adjustments if the source repository has not been updated.\n\n## Managing legitimate differences\nSome fields are intentionally changed by Kubernetes controllers. Configure appropriate diff customization only after identifying the field owner and reason for the difference. Do not hide broad differences simply to obtain a Synced status; doing so can conceal real configuration errors.\n\n## Incident response\nInspect the diff, identify who changed the field, and compare audit events with Git history. If an emergency change is valid, record it in Git before reconciliation restores the previous state. Verify that the corrected desired configuration is synchronized and healthy."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-10",
        "title": "Resource Health Assessment and Application Status",
        "content": "# Resource Health Assessment and Application Status\n\n## Status dimensions\nArgo CD reports synchronization state and health state separately. Synced indicates that the live resources match the rendered desired configuration. Healthy indicates that the resources satisfy their health assessment. An application can be Synced but Degraded, or OutOfSync while its currently running workloads appear healthy.\n\n## Resource health signals\nDeployments, StatefulSets, DaemonSets, Pods, Services, and custom resources expose different readiness and availability signals. A Deployment may remain progressing while replicas are unavailable. A custom resource may need a custom health assessment when its controller reports status in a format Argo CD does not interpret automatically.\n\n## Operational diagnosis\nUse `argocd app get <application>` and inspect individual resource status. Follow with `kubectl describe` and relevant Pod logs to understand scheduling, image pulling, readiness, or controller failures. Check events and conditions rather than relying only on the overall dashboard color.\n\n## Health customization\nIf a custom resource is incorrectly classified, review the health assessment support for that resource kind and the installed Argo CD version. Any custom health logic should represent meaningful operational readiness. Test it against progressing, healthy, degraded, and missing-resource conditions before using it for release gates."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-11",
        "title": "Sync Waves, Hooks, and Deployment Ordering",
        "content": "# Sync Waves, Hooks, and Deployment Ordering\n\n## Deployment dependency problem\nSome Kubernetes resources must exist before others can operate correctly. For example, a namespace and configuration resource may need to exist before an application Deployment starts. Argo CD sync waves provide ordering metadata so resources can be applied in a planned sequence.\n\n## Sync waves\nResources can be assigned wave numbers using the Argo CD sync-wave annotation. Lower-numbered waves are processed before higher-numbered waves. Argo CD evaluates resource health between phases, which helps coordinate dependent components. Ordering should reflect actual dependencies rather than arbitrary preferences.\n\n## Hooks\nPreSync, Sync, and PostSync hooks provide lifecycle actions around synchronization. They can run database migrations, validation jobs, or post-deployment checks. Hook deletion policies and retry behavior must be designed carefully so failed jobs do not block future releases indefinitely.\n\n## Troubleshooting\nInspect sync operation details to identify the wave or hook that failed. A migration Job may fail because of database connectivity, incompatible schema changes, or insufficient permissions. Validate hook idempotency and ensure that retrying a failed sync cannot corrupt application data. Use a tested backward-compatible migration strategy for production releases."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-12",
        "title": "Application Sync Operations and Change Review",
        "content": "# Application Sync Operations and Change Review\n\n## Sync operation lifecycle\nA synchronization operation applies the rendered desired manifests to the destination cluster. Before execution, operators should review the resource diff, target revision, destination, and any resource deletion or replacement. A sync operation can partially apply resources before a later resource fails, so the resulting cluster state must be checked after an unsuccessful operation.\n\n## Change review\nUse pull requests to review manifest changes and require appropriate approvals for production. Review image tags, environment values, resource limits, service exposure, security context, and persistent storage changes. A small Git commit can still cause a large deployment impact if it changes a shared base or chart default.\n\n## Execution and verification\nUse the Argo CD UI, CLI, or approved automation to initiate synchronization. Record the revision and operation result. After synchronization, verify resource health, application endpoints, logs, and business-level smoke tests. A successful sync alone does not prove that the application works correctly.\n\n## Failure handling\nIf an operation fails, inspect the failed resource and operation history before retrying. Correct the source manifest or underlying dependency, then synchronize the corrected revision. Avoid repeated blind retries when a migration, hook, or destructive resource change may already have executed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-13",
        "title": "Git-Based Rollback and Release Recovery",
        "content": "# Git-Based Rollback and Release Recovery\n\n## Rollback principle\nIn GitOps, the durable recovery action is normally to restore a previously approved desired configuration in Git. Reverting a commit or promoting a known-good release revision makes the recovery reproducible and visible to reviewers. A temporary live-cluster edit is not a durable rollback because reconciliation may replace it.\n\n## Recovery preparation\nMaintain identifiable release tags or commit references and retain the manifests and chart versions needed to reproduce a deployment. Database schema changes, persistent data, and external service changes may not be reversible by simply restoring an older Deployment image.\n\n## Incident procedure\nIdentify the last known-good revision, compare it with the failed revision, and determine whether the failure includes data or infrastructure changes. Revert or select the approved revision through the normal review process. Allow Argo CD to reconcile the destination, then verify workload readiness and application behavior.\n\n## Post-rollback validation\nCheck the Application sync and health states, Pod readiness, service routing, logs, and critical transactions. Document the cause and preserve the failed revision for analysis. If the rollback cannot restore compatibility because of an irreversible schema change, use a forward-fix recovery plan rather than repeatedly applying an incompatible old release."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-14",
        "title": "Application Secrets and Repository Credential Security",
        "content": "# Application Secrets and Repository Credential Security\n\n## Separate application secrets from Git configuration\nKubernetes Secret objects contain sensitive values, but base64 encoding is not encryption. Committing plain Secret manifests to an unrestricted repository exposes credentials to anyone who can read the repository history. Repository credentials used by Argo CD are also distinct from secrets consumed by the deployed application.\n\n## Secure secret delivery\nUse an approved secret-management approach such as an external secrets controller, a cloud secret manager integration, or encrypted secret manifests with controlled decryption. Restrict Kubernetes RBAC access to Secret objects and enable encryption at rest in the cluster where supported. Rotate credentials and define ownership and expiration procedures.\n\n## Repository access\nUse narrowly scoped deploy keys, tokens, or supported authentication mechanisms. Avoid sharing a single powerful credential across unrelated teams. Ensure that logs, command history, CI output, and Application manifests do not expose tokens.\n\n## Incident response\nIf a credential is committed or leaked, revoke or rotate it immediately, assess repository and cluster access logs, and identify affected applications. Removing the value from the latest commit is insufficient if it remains in Git history. Validate the replacement credential and confirm that unauthorized access has been prevented."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-15",
        "title": "Application Resource Requests, Limits, and Quotas",
        "content": "# Application Resource Requests, Limits, and Quotas\n\n## Workload resource configuration\nKubernetes containers can define CPU and memory requests and limits. Requests influence scheduling because the scheduler uses them when selecting nodes. Limits constrain resource consumption, although CPU throttling and memory termination have different operational behavior. These settings should be version-controlled with the workload manifests.\n\n## Namespace governance\nResourceQuota can restrict aggregate resource consumption in a namespace. LimitRange can define default or permitted resource requests and limits for individual containers. These controls help prevent one application team from consuming all cluster capacity, but overly restrictive policies can also prevent workloads from starting.\n\n## Sizing process\nUse observed CPU and memory usage, traffic patterns, startup behavior, and peak load to select initial values. Set requests to support reliable scheduling and limits to protect shared capacity. Review JVM memory, sidecar overhead, and burst workloads rather than copying arbitrary values between services.\n\n## Troubleshooting\nA Pod stuck in Pending may exceed available requested capacity or violate quota. OOMKilled indicates memory pressure or an insufficient memory limit, while CPU throttling may cause latency without restarting the container. Inspect Pod events, node capacity, quota usage, and container metrics before changing resource values."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-16",
        "title": "Deployment Probes and Rollout Readiness",
        "content": "# Deployment Probes and Rollout Readiness\n\n## Probe responsibilities\nA startup probe gives a slow-starting application time to initialize. A readiness probe determines whether a container should receive Service traffic. A liveness probe detects a process that should be restarted. These probes solve different problems and should not all use an identical endpoint or timing configuration.\n\n## Rollout behavior\nA Deployment rollout depends on updated Pods becoming available according to its strategy and readiness conditions. Incorrect readiness checks can send traffic to an application that is not prepared. Aggressive liveness checks can repeatedly restart a slow but healthy process and prevent it from completing startup.\n\n## Implementation\nChoose endpoints that represent the intended condition. Readiness should validate the ability to serve requests; liveness should avoid depending on temporary external services when that would cause restart storms. Configure initial delays, timeouts, periods, and failure thresholds based on measured startup and recovery behavior.\n\n## Troubleshooting\nInspect Pod events and probe failure messages. Test the endpoint from inside the container and verify its port, path, protocol, and response code. Review application logs for initialization failures. During a rollout, check Deployment conditions and unavailable replicas before assuming Argo CD synchronization itself is broken."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-14-section-17",
        "title": "Application Resource Troubleshooting and Controller Diagnostics",
        "content": "# Application Resource Troubleshooting and Controller Diagnostics\n\n## Troubleshooting layers\nAn Argo CD application deployment depends on several components: repository access, manifest generation, Application and AppProject validation, cluster connectivity, Kubernetes admission, resource scheduling, and workload health. Identify the failing layer before changing configuration. A dashboard status is a symptom, not a complete root-cause explanation.\n\n## Evidence collection\nCapture the Application specification, target revision, sync status, health status, operation history, resource diff, and relevant conditions. Inspect the repository-server logs for fetch or rendering problems, application-controller logs for reconciliation problems, and destination cluster events for admission or scheduling failures.\n\n## Diagnostic workflow\nUse `argocd app get <application>` and `argocd app diff <application>` to inspect application state and desired-versus-live differences. Use `kubectl get`, `kubectl describe`, and targeted logs against the destination cluster. Confirm the exact resource name, namespace, revision, and timestamp so evidence from separate incidents is not mixed.\n\n## Recovery and prevention\nFix the root cause in the appropriate source: repository configuration, chart or Kustomize manifests, project policy, cluster credentials, or workload settings. Reconcile through the approved GitOps process and verify health after recovery. Add monitoring for repeated sync failures, degraded applications, repository errors, and controller saturation."
      }
    ]
  },
